1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,904
Here's a practical question. When you fold many specialized models into one, how hard should you work to do it well?

2
00:00:07,084 --> 00:00:19,588
The answer isn't a single method — it's a budget. Sometimes a cheap, one-shot merge is enough; sometimes it pays to spend real computation to refine it. This video is about knowing which, in advance.

3
00:00:20,008 --> 00:00:31,336
Start simple. You take one pre-trained model and fine-tune it separately for many tasks — an expert for everyday photos, one for satellite images, one for digits, and so on.

4
00:00:31,516 --> 00:00:39,604
They all began from the same checkpoint, and only drifted apart during fine-tuning. That shared origin is what makes combining them possible.

5
00:00:39,784 --> 00:00:48,976
Serving a whole zoo of experts is expensive, and you often can't retrain one model on everything — the data may be private, or simply gone.

6
00:00:49,156 --> 00:00:59,812
Model consolidation is the fix: fold those experts back into a single model — same size, same cost — that handles every task, with no joint retraining.

7
00:01:00,232 --> 00:01:05,536
Here's the idea that ties every method together. They are all chasing the same target.

8
00:01:05,716 --> 00:01:16,828
For each task, on that task's own inputs, the merged model should reproduce the representation its expert would produce. Write that down, and you get one clean functional objective.

9
00:01:17,008 --> 00:01:24,808
No labels are needed — the target is the expert's own output. Every method is just a different way to approximate this one objective.

10
00:01:25,228 --> 00:01:31,444
And this isn't a binary choice. It's a spectrum, from cheap and approximate to costly and exact.

11
00:01:31,624 --> 00:01:42,496
At the cheap end, closed-form merging collects a few statistics and solves one equation. Methods like RegMean finish in under a minute — but they only approximate the goal, leaving a fixed gap.

12
00:01:42,676 --> 00:01:52,468
In the middle sit two intermediate points: a Gauss-Newton step that restores cross-layer coupling, and a block-wise variant that refines a few layers at a time to stay within a memory budget.

13
00:01:52,648 --> 00:02:07,048
At the far end is end-to-end refinement — our E2E-Merge — which optimizes the objective directly. More expensive, but it closes the gap. Each rung restores more of the deployed function, at a higher cost.

14
00:02:07,468 --> 00:02:12,772
So when is it worth climbing that spectrum? That's the central question: when does refinement pay?

15
00:02:12,952 --> 00:02:23,464
The cheap merge leaves a permanent gap. Refinement can close it, but it has to estimate and optimize the objective from finite data — and that costs data and compute.

16
00:02:23,644 --> 00:02:37,708
Refinement pays exactly when those costs fall below the gap the cheap merge leaves behind. Below that break-even point, the cheap merge is the better bet. Let's watch it along three axes — data, compute, and memory.

17
00:02:38,128 --> 00:02:42,520
First, data. How many unlabeled examples per task do you have?

18
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:51,100
With very few — thirty-two or sixty-four — the cheap RegMean merge is simply better. Refinement doesn't have enough to learn from.

19
00:02:51,280 --> 00:03:01,696
But the lines cross at a hundred and twenty-eight examples. Past that, refinement pulls ahead and keeps climbing — to about eighty-eight and a half percent at a thousand examples per task.

20
00:03:01,876 --> 00:03:07,804
The lesson: below about a hundred examples, don't pay for refinement — the closed form wins.

21
00:03:08,224 --> 00:03:11,872
Second, compute — how long you let refinement run.

22
00:03:12,052 --> 00:03:22,036
At two hundred and fifty-six examples per task, refinement overtakes the cheap merge almost immediately — within the first five hundred steps, about a minute of extra work.

23
00:03:22,216 --> 00:03:29,248
From there it keeps improving, but with clearly diminishing returns: most of the gain arrives by two thousand steps.

24
00:03:29,428 --> 00:03:32,884
So a modest compute budget already captures most of the win.

25
00:03:33,304 --> 00:03:39,472
Third, memory. Refinement can run block by block, trading peak memory for accuracy.

26
00:03:39,652 --> 00:03:45,388
Refine just one block at a time and you stay under four gigabytes, already beating the cheap baseline.

27
00:03:45,568 --> 00:03:57,760
Group more blocks together and both memory and accuracy climb — up to the full run at about ninety percent and eight gigabytes. On a larger model, the same dial runs up to about thirty gigabytes.

28
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:02,212
So even on a modest GPU, you can pick the point on this curve that fits your hardware.

29
00:04:02,632 --> 00:04:08,632
Now the most useful part. You don't have to guess where you fall on this trade-off — you can measure it, before spending anything.

30
00:04:08,812 --> 00:04:18,124
Fit the cheap merge, then read one number: how far its outputs still sit from the experts, on held-out inputs. No labels, no test data.

31
00:04:18,304 --> 00:04:28,912
Across all seventy four-task combinations, that single residual tracks the eventual refinement gain almost perfectly — a rank correlation of zero point nine six.

32
00:04:29,092 --> 00:04:42,340
Turned into a decision — refine, or stop — it is right about ninety-three percent of the time, far beyond what accuracy or task similarity can tell you. Measure first, then only pay for refinement where it will pay back.

33
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:48,208
So what happens at the generous end of the budget, where refinement gets everything it needs?

34
00:04:48,388 --> 00:04:59,020
On eight image tasks with a large vision model, it reaches ninety-four point two percent — essentially the accuracy of the eight separately fine-tuned experts, in a single model.

35
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:08,968
And the harder the problem, the more it pays. The gain over the best cheap baseline grows from two and a half points at two experts, to six at eight, and nine and a half at twenty.

36
00:05:09,148 --> 00:05:17,236
The advantage grows with the number of experts, and on weaker backbones — precisely the settings where a one-shot formula struggles most.

37
00:05:17,656 --> 00:05:22,744
Why does spending that compute help? Because small errors at each layer compound through depth.

38
00:05:22,924 --> 00:05:33,892
Two methods can look equally accurate layer by layer, yet one ends up three times as far from the expert at the output. Only an end-to-end objective sees, and fixes, that final gap.

39
00:05:34,072 --> 00:05:46,408
And to stay honest: on a language benchmark with different readouts, the cheap RegMean method wins every paired run. The break-even points are guidance tied to this setting, not universal guarantees.

40
00:05:46,828 --> 00:05:55,132
So here's the takeaway. Merging and refinement aren't rivals — they're points on one spectrum, all approximating the same functional goal.

41
00:05:55,312 --> 00:06:01,912
Match each expert's behavior. Then choose how far to climb by what you can afford — data, compute, and memory.

42
00:06:02,092 --> 00:06:07,972
And you can make that call before you train: the cheap merge's own residual tells you whether refinement will pay.

43
00:06:08,152 --> 00:06:13,480
The paper and code are coming soon, linked alongside this video. Thanks for watching.

